  "worked_solution": "The general solution to the given differential equation is\n\n$$\nV(t)={V}_{0}{e}^{-rt},\n$$\n\nwhere ${V}_{0}$ is the value when $t=0.$\n\nLet ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the value equals $\\frac{1}{5}{V}_{0}.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{5}{V}_{0}\\,\u21d2\\,{V}_{0}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}}=\\frac{1}{5}{V}_{0}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for ${t}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{0}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{5}{V}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{V}_{0}}{e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cancel{{V}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{-r{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061({e}^{-r{t}_{1}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\ -r{t}_{1} & =-\\operatorname{ln}\u20615 \\\\ {t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u20615}{r}\\,\\,(\u2217)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate $r.$ We're told that the value reduces by one-half after the first year. Therefore,\n\n$$\nV(1)=\\frac{1}{2}{V}_{0}\\,\u21d2\\,{V}_{0}{e}^{-r}=\\frac{1}{2}{V}_{0}.\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation for $r,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{0}{e}^{-r} & =\\frac{1}{2}{V}_{0} \\\\ \\cancel{{V}_{0}}{e}^{-r} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cancel{{V}_{0}} \\\\ {e}^{-r} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}\u2061({e}^{-r}) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ -r & =-\\operatorname{ln}\u20612 \\\\ r & =\\operatorname{ln}\u20612\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this into $(\u2217)$ and evaluating, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}_{1} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u20615}{r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u20615}{\\operatorname{ln}\u20612} \\\\  & \u22482.3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.",
